Decisions: SDD

Purpose:
  Define how human decisions are requested, recorded, approved, rejected,
  superseded and applied inside the SDD system. This is the layer that
  manages the POINTS where AI needs a human decision — it is not a place
  where AI records its own decisions (see Note below for the full
  distinction; this is the single most important thing to understand about
  this file).

  This file does not hold concrete project decisions itself (e.g. "should
  payment use PostgreSQL"). Those are decision INSTANCES, filed under
  pending/approved/rejected/superseded once raised (see Storage below).
  This file only defines the rules that govern that lifecycle — the same
  "engine, not instance data" pattern already used by tasks/tasks.sdd,
  chains/chains.sdd and skills/skills.sdd.


Owns:
  ./


Scope:
  Decision lifecycle
  Decision structure (contract every decision instance must satisfy)
  Approval / rejection rules
  Conflict resolution
  Decision traceability
  Decision state (instance-level — see States below)


DoesNotOwn:
  Project-specific architecture (see project/architecture.sdd)
  Project-specific requirements (see project/)
  Tasks (see tasks/tasks.sdd)
  Skills (see skills/skills.sdd)
  Chains (see chains/chains.sdd)

  (this file records THAT a decision was needed and WHAT was decided — it
  does not own the domains a decision happens to be ABOUT; see
  architecture/architecture.sdd -> Forbidden for the SDD-system-level
  version of this same boundary)


Storage:
  (directory convention only — no concrete decision instances exist yet in
  this replay, see Note below)

  pending:
    path: ./pending

  approved:
    path: ./approved

  rejected:
    path: ./rejected

  superseded:
    path: ./superseded


DecisionTypes:

  architecture
  requirement
  security
  dependency
  technology
  scope
  process
  exception


DecisionLifecycle:

  CREATED
    >
  ANALYZED
    >
  PENDING
    >
  APPROVED

  CREATED
    >
  ANALYZED
    >
  PENDING
    >
  REJECTED

  APPROVED
    >
  SUPERSEDED


DecisionContract:

  Every decision instance MUST define:

    id
    type
    question
    reason
    context
    alternatives
    impact
    affected
    requested_by
    state
    resolution


Rules:

  [D1]
  AI MUST request a decision when an unresolved human choice
  affects architecture, scope, security or critical behavior.

  [D2]
  AI MUST NOT silently convert an unresolved conflict into
  an implementation decision.

  [D3]
  AI MUST provide the context required for the human to decide.

  [D4]
  AI SHOULD provide possible alternatives when known.

  [D5]
  Every approved decision MUST be traceable to the work
  that caused it.

  [D6]
  Every rejected decision MUST preserve its history.

  [D7]
  A superseded decision MUST NOT remain an active rule.

  [D8]
  A decision MAY affect project model, architecture,
  chain or task execution.

  [D9]
  Decision impact MUST be evaluated before implementation.

  [D10]
  AI MUST NOT modify unrelated project areas because of
  a decision without impact analysis.

  [D11]
  Decisions MUST remain immutable after approval.

  [D12]
  A new decision MUST supersede an old decision instead of
  silently rewriting its history.


DecisionImpact:

  local:
    affects one project component

  domain:
    affects one domain

  project:
    affects the project architecture or behavior

  global:
    affects the SDD system itself


Escalation:
  (mirrors PROJECT.sdd -> HumanDecision — this section does not redefine
  that rule, it only names the decision-layer-specific triggers that make
  [D1] fire)

  AI_CAN_DECIDE:
    routine work
    deterministic work

  AI_MUST_ASK:
    architecture conflict
    security exception
    scope change
    major technology decision
    business ambiguity
    global rule change


Resolution:

  APPROVED:
    referenced work MAY continue.

  REJECTED:
    related work MUST follow the rejection result.

  SUPERSEDED:
    the previous decision MUST NOT be used as active authority.


Traceability:

  Prompt
    >
  Analysis
    >
  Decision
    >
  Task
    >
  Chain
    >
  Implementation
    >
  Verification


Navigation:
  project: @project
  architecture: @architecture
  chains: @chains
  skills: @skills
  prompts: @prompts
  tasks: @tasks
  state: @state


States:
  (mirrors the canonical legend in PROJECT.sdd -> State, reinterpreted for
  a single decision instance's lifecycle; `^` is the one addition — see
  Note below for why)

  ? = PENDING     (canonical "decision required" — awaiting a human choice)
  ~ = ANALYZING   (canonical "active" — context/alternatives being assembled)
  + = APPROVED    (canonical "completed" — human confirmed, dependent work may continue)
  - = REJECTED    (canonical "failed" — human declined, history preserved)
  ! = BLOCKED     (canonical "blocked" — cannot be analyzed yet, e.g. missing context)
  > = NEXT        (canonical "next" — queued to be raised with the human next)
  @ = REFERENCE   (canonical "reference" — pointer to a decision recorded elsewhere)
  ^ = SUPERSEDED  (additive — not part of the canonical 7-glyph legend, added
                    because no canonical glyph covers "was approved, then
                    overridden by a newer decision"; see Note)


State: +
  (STEP 9 completed — decisions.sdd router (Rules [D1]-[D12]) + four Storage
  directories (pending/approved/rejected/superseded, each a `.gitkeep`
  placeholder) created. No concrete decision instances exist yet — see
  Note below, same restraint as STEP 5/6/7/8.)


Note:
  (prompt/new/29.md, STEP 9) Header kept as `Decisions: SDD` rather than the
  source's proposed `Spec: DecisionSystem`, for the same reason
  `tasks/tasks.sdd` kept `TaskEngine: SDD` over its own source's `Spec:
  TaskSystem`: the `<Name>: SDD` pattern (`Architecture: SDD`, `Chains:
  SDD`, `Skills: SDD`, `Prompts: SDD`) is this repo's actual naming
  convention across sibling root engine files, and "apply, don't copy"
  means the existing convention wins over a per-chunk proposal.

  `Owns`/`Scope`/`DoesNotOwn` were restructured from the source: the source
  used `Owns:` for a list of abstract concepts (decision lifecycle,
  structure, etc.), which collides with how `Owns:` is used elsewhere in
  this repo (a path, e.g. `tasks/tasks.sdd -> Owns: ./`). That concept list
  was moved to a new `Scope:` field instead, and `Owns:` now holds the path
  — the same collision-avoidance move `tasks/tasks.sdd` made when it
  renamed its own `Scope:` to `TaskScope:` to avoid colliding with the
  file's top-level `Scope:`.

  The `States:` legend was reworked from the source's proposal. The source
  reassigned `+`/`-`/`~`/`?` to decision-specific labels and introduced an
  undocumented `^` glyph while dropping `!`/`>` — but a fresh read of
  `tasks/tasks.sdd -> States` showed the actual precedent is to MIRROR the
  canonical 7-glyph legend from `PROJECT.sdd -> State` (same glyphs, same
  underlying meaning, reinterpreted for the instance type at hand) and only
  ADD a new glyph when a real gap exists, documenting the addition
  explicitly — exactly how `tasks.sdd` added `@ = reference` to its own
  States block and noted in prose that this was "not a new global marker,
  just locally completed." This file follows the same discipline: `?`/`~`/
  `+`/`-`/`!`/`>`/`@` all keep their canonical meaning, reinterpreted for a
  decision instance, and `^ = SUPERSEDED` is added on top as an explicit,
  justified extension, because none of the canonical 7 glyphs cover "was
  approved, then overridden by a newer decision."

  As with STEP 5/6/7/8, this step is deliberately skeleton + rules engine
  only. The source's `DEC-004.sdd` walkthrough (a Frontend-vs-Database
  architecture question, resolved `? -> +` after human approval of
  alternative "A: FE -> API -> BE -> DB") was NOT created as a real file
  under `pending/` — there is no concrete project domain yet to raise a
  real decision about (`project/` remains schema-only, STEP 4). It is kept
  here only as an illustration of the shape a decision instance takes once
  one exists: `Decision: DEC-004`, `Type: architecture`, `Question:`,
  `Reason:`, `Context: @project/<domain>/architecture.sdd`, `Alternatives:`
  (A/B/...), `Impact:`, `Affected: @project/<domain>`, `State: ?`,
  `RequestedBy: @task/<id>` — moving to `State: +` under `approved/` once a
  human picks an alternative.

  Most important principle (source's closing point, reformulated): this
  directory is not "AI's own decision folder." It is the layer that manages
  the points where AI needs a human decision — the mechanism that keeps
  `AI CAN DECIDE` (routine, deterministic work) separate from `AI MUST ASK`
  (architecture conflict, security exception, scope change, major
  technology decision, business ambiguity, global rule change; see
  `Escalation:` above, which applies `PROJECT.sdd -> HumanDecision` to this
  layer without redefining it).
